WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.669
This video is made possible by CuriosityStream.

00:00:03.669 --> 00:00:08.930
Get the CuriosityStream/Nebula bundle deal,
on sale now for $15 a year, to watch our brand

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:15.500
new Nebula Original Documentary at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:20.599
In March, 2020, the cruise industry shut down.

00:00:20.599 --> 00:00:25.849
There are no qualifiers to that sentence—it’s
not that certain ships from certain companies

00:00:25.849 --> 00:00:27.580
in certain countries shut down.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:34.320
It’s that every ship from every company
in every country stopped accepting passengers

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.390
in March, 2020.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.140
That’s… unprecedented.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:46.050
Never before, in modern times, has there been
such a complete, global shut down of an industry

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:47.550
of this size.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.780
This isn’t like airlines or restaurants
or movie theaters.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:55.050
They couldn’t keep operating at a certain
level or with certain precautions or in certain

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.460
geographies—every single cruise ship shut
down.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.220
The second that happened, though, time started
ticking.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:08.789
Cruise lines were on the clock with the biggest
challenge they might ever face: they had to

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:14.380
survive as organizations with no revenue,
no passengers, and little-to-no government

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:19.030
support until it was safe and legal to sail
again.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:25.710
As it turns out, doing nothing, as a company,
is actually quite a lot of work.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:31.049
After ships dropped off their last loads of
passengers, thousands of people remained onboard:

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:32.310
the crew.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.979
Naturally, management started to wonder what
they should do with them.

00:01:35.979 --> 00:01:38.680
At first, the answer was nothing.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.859
You see, as the shutdown started, much of
the global cruise fleet was in the Caribbean,

00:01:43.859 --> 00:01:47.549
finishing up the peak season before crossing
the Atlantic for the summer Mediterranean

00:01:47.549 --> 00:01:48.720
cruise season.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.539
As most Caribbean cruises leave from the US,
that meant that the American CDC’s March

00:01:53.539 --> 00:01:58.630
14th no-sail order was what officially prevented
them from sailing—even if in practice most

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.469
shut down voluntarily.

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:05.139
That no-sail order, however, was initially
slated to expire after just thirty days, so

00:02:05.139 --> 00:02:09.390
most cruise lines decided to leave their ships
staffed and wait it out, under the hope that

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.960
they could salvage their summer seasons.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:18.480
On April 9th, though, the CDC extended the
order for 100 days, until July 24th, making

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.440
it very clear that this would not be a short-term
stoppage.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.640
In most cases, cruise ships are subject to
the laws of the countries in which they’re

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.549
registered, which means that most cruise lines
register their vessels in countries with loose

00:02:31.549 --> 00:02:35.280
labor laws like Panama, the Bahamas, or Malta.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.920
That typically lets them hire from anywhere,
which in practice gives them access to low-wage

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.519
labor pools from places like Eastern Europe
or the Philippines.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:49.000
Now, cruise ships are, of course, massive,
and correspondently need a massive number

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.870
of staff to run.

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:57.100
The Carnival Panorama, for example, has a
crew size of 1,450 people, and Carnival, its

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:00.250
owner, has some 23 ships.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:05.220
That meant that, once they decided to officially
pull the plug in April, 2020, they had some

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:11.450
26,000 staff members from all around the world
who each needed to get home.

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:16.150
In the era of pared-back flight schedules,
border closures, and complex testing and quarantine

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:22.980
protocols, it soon became very clear that
finding 26,000 flight itineraries for 26,000

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.939
stranded crew members would not be a quick
and simple exercise.

00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:35.950
So, rather than 26,000 itineraries, they cut
it back to just about seven.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:40.980
In late-April, 18 of Carnival’s 23 ships
started sailing to a spot off the coast of

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.670
Grand Bahama Island.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:48.720
Then, once together, over a few days in early
May, some 10,000 crew members shuffled between

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.030
the various ships using tenders.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:55.579
The task entailed more or less grouping the
crew into geographic regions—south-east

00:03:55.579 --> 00:04:00.709
Asian crew boarded the Carnival Conquest,
south-Asian crew boarded the Ecstasy, eastern-European

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:04.019
crew boarded the Magic, and so on and so forth.

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:09.740
Once the crew transfer was completed, on May
4th, seven ships—the Liberty, Dream, Magic,

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.220
Breeze, Ecstasy, Conquest, and Glory—left.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:17.620
The Carnival Glory had the shortest journey
of them all—tasked with repatriating many

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.530
of the Caribbean crew-members to their homes.

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:24.779
Just five days after departing, it docked
in St Lucia, then the next day in St Vincent

00:04:24.779 --> 00:04:30.230
&amp; The Grenadines, then St George’s, Barbados,
and so on and so forth—completing a multi-week

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:32.810
milk-run of the West Indies.

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:37.139
Next to complete its mission was the Carnival
Breeze, dropping British crew off in Southampton,

00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:41.620
while the Carnival Magic made a quick stop
in Gibraltar before arriving in Dubrovnik,

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:45.360
Croatia to repatriate much of Carnival’s
eastern-European crew.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.639
Meanwhile, the journeys of the Liberty, Dream,
Conquest, and Ecstasy were just getting started

00:04:50.639 --> 00:04:55.920
as they each stopped at various South African
ports to re-stock, refuel, and drop off African

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:56.970
crew-members.

00:04:56.970 --> 00:05:01.160
After two weeks of sailing the Indian Ocean,
the Dream and Conquest arrived in the waters

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:05.650
off the coast of Denpasar to bring many of
the Indonesian crew home, while the Liberty

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.949
arrived in Mumbai, India.

00:05:06.949 --> 00:05:11.860
The Dream then made a quick journey west to
Jakarta to drop off more of its Indonesian

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:17.620
crew, just as the Ecstasy made it to Mumbai
as well, carrying even more Indian crew members.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.419
Then, as soon as the Conquest met the Dream
in Jakarta, the two sailed together to Manila

00:05:22.419 --> 00:05:27.360
to drop off a huge number of Philippine staff
members, while the Liberty and Ecstasy sailed

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.610
south to anchor off the coast of Colombo and
drop off Sri Lankan crew.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:36.750
With that, as June turned into July, this
repatriation mission was complete, with some

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:42.870
crew having spent two months sailing from
the Bahamas to get back home.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:47.410
While getting these 26,000 crew members off
their boats and off their payroll was a major

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:52.229
concern of management at Carnival once the
long-term nature of the shut-down became clear,

00:05:52.229 --> 00:05:57.210
attention, among all cruise line management
teams, quickly refocused onto how to actually

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:00.900
survive as a business when there’s no business
to be made.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:06.650
In the third quarter of 2019, each of the
three largest cruise companies made billions

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:07.650
in profits.

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:13.669
In the same quarter one year later, Carnival
earned $31 million in revenue, Norwegian $6.5

00:06:13.669 --> 00:06:19.690
million, and Royal Caribbean managed to earn
-$33 million in revenue—something almost

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:21.970
unheard of among large companies.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:29.340
So, how you do survive as a company when you
quite literally have no way to make money?

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:34.170
Well, cruise lines did have one thing going
for them.

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:38.349
Prior to COVID, they were highly, highly profitable.

00:06:38.349 --> 00:06:44.120
In fact, many often pocketed hundreds of dollars
in profit per passenger.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:48.400
In addition, cruising is a highly cyclical
industry.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.050
What that means is that when the overall economy
is good, the cruise business is quite good,

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:58.550
while when the overall economy is bad, the
cruise business is quite bad.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:03.919
That's because, when money gets tight for
a person, one of the first things to go is

00:07:03.919 --> 00:07:04.919
vacation.

00:07:04.919 --> 00:07:09.729
Therefore, cruise lines are used to weathering
out leaner times, so they traditionally keep

00:07:09.729 --> 00:07:12.770
quite a lot of money in their war chests.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:17.360
At the end of the third financial quarter
of 2020, by which time the companies had unlocked

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.789
more liquid assets and passed the initial
shock phase of the shutdown, Norwegian Cruise

00:07:21.789 --> 00:07:27.699
Line Holdings, the third-largest cruise conglomerate
in the world, had some $2.4 billion in the

00:07:27.699 --> 00:07:28.699
bank.

00:07:28.699 --> 00:07:33.710
Meanwhile, the Royal Caribbean Group had about
$3 billion in cash, while the Carnival Group,

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:39.419
by far the largest cruise company in the world,
held about $8.2 billion in cash.

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:44.789
That was what’s referred to as their “runway,”
and the task was to get their burn-rate, how

00:07:44.789 --> 00:07:49.680
much they spend per day to stay in existence,
to its lowest possible level.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.880
In the case of Carnival, if they spent $50
million per day, for example, they would only

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:57.919
survive some 164 days without revenue.

00:07:57.919 --> 00:08:03.810
If they were able to get that down to only
$25 million a day, though, they’d have 328

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:07.449
days, almost a year, of runway.

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:12.169
Once the long-term nature of the shutdown
became clear, furloughing or firing almost

00:08:12.169 --> 00:08:18.300
all on-ship staff and a sizable chunk of onshore
staff was an easy decision for cruise line

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.300
management.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:24.919
One of the tougher decisions was what to do
with their key assets: the ships.

00:08:24.919 --> 00:08:27.419
Unlike people, they couldn’t just let them
go.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:33.880
In the case of Carnival, their oldest, smallest,
and cheapest ship—the Ecstasy—cost some

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:38.930
$275 million to build back in 1991.

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:45.310
Nowadays, they’re building $780 million
ships like the Carnival Panorama.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:50.529
Put all together, this means a cruise line’s
fleet is incredibly valuable, and since the

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:56.140
ships weren’t carrying passengers around
the world, they had to put them somewhere.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:09:00.880
However, as it turns out, both the world’s
cruise ports and the world’s cruise ships

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.730
weren’t built with a total industry shutdown
in mind.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:09.830
First of all, you can’t just turn off a
cruise ship, tie it up, and lock the door

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:10.970
behind you.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:16.100
With very little exception, from the moment
a ship enters service to the moment it’s

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:21.269
decommissioned, there are people onboard as
these massive machines require constant monitoring

00:09:21.269 --> 00:09:23.990
and maintenance to stay in working order.

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:28.640
In addition, cruise ports were built to suit
the need, and since the early days of the

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:33.980
industry, the need has been for ships to stop
every week or so to unload passengers, restock,

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.000
and load back up.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:39.430
They’re designed for the majority of ships
to be at sea which historically was fine,

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:42.120
because the majority of ships were always
at sea.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:46.730
Now, however, that means there just isn’t
enough space in the world’s cruise ports

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:50.279
for every cruise ship to tie up and wait things
out.

00:09:50.279 --> 00:09:55.500
In fact, there’s not even close to enough
space, and most ports charge ships tens of

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:00.400
thousands of dollars per day to tie up, so
the vast majority of the world’s cruise

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:03.510
fleet is out at sea… floating around.

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:08.610
They each now carry an average of about 100
crew members—a fraction of their normal

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:13.639
staffing levels—performing the most essential
functions—navigation, maintenance, engineering,

00:10:13.639 --> 00:10:16.839
food preparation, medical care, and more.

00:10:16.839 --> 00:10:21.560
In the case of Carnival, 15 of their 23 ships
are currently anchored off the coast of the

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:26.510
Bahamas, and about once a week, one of them
will sail to Miami to pick up food, mail,

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:30.810
and other supplies, which they then transfer
between the ships upon return.

00:10:30.810 --> 00:10:35.420
Most lines are operating with some version
of this floating cluster approach as it’s

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:39.990
clearly one of the lowest-cost methods for
storage that keeps ships in an adequate state

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:43.740
of readiness for when cruising finally returns.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:49.050
And that’s the key question right now: when
will it going to be possible for cruising

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:50.730
to return?

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:54.580
In Summer, 2020, some thought the answer was
then.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:59.180
Particularly in Europe, which had a robust
summer travel season, quite a few smaller

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:05.040
cruise ships and even some larger ones brought
passengers back onboard, with precautions.

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:09.850
In general, this meant the ships were capacity
capped and passengers had to be tested before

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:14.410
boarding, were subject to temperature checks,
were required to wear a mask in communal areas,

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:17.380
and could only go ashore on managed excursions.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:21.070
With these and other rules, cruise companies
thought they might have cracked the code of

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:26.600
sailing safely and profitably during a pandemic,
but then reality hit.

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:32.389
On the MS Roald Amundsen—an expedition cruise
ship that typically carries 500 or so passengers

00:11:32.389 --> 00:11:38.279
through the Arctic or Antarctic—62 people
tested positive for COVID after a trip around

00:11:38.279 --> 00:11:43.050
Svalbard, putting stress on the contact-tracing
system of northern Norway.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:47.910
Elsewhere in Norway, the SeaDream 1 had to
cancel a cruise after a positive COVID test,

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:53.160
and then a few months later, the very same
ship left Barbados on the very first cruise

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:57.709
in the Caribbean since the start of the pandemic,
which soon had to be cancelled due to nine

00:11:57.709 --> 00:12:00.660
passengers and crew testing positive.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:05.310
On the other side of the continent, UnCruise
adventures left Juneau in early August on

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:10.070
the very first cruise with passengers from
an American port since March, 2020, until

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:14.279
a passenger’s test came back positive, forcing
them to return to port, put all passengers

00:12:14.279 --> 00:12:18.329
under quarantine, and cancel the rest of their
season.

00:12:18.329 --> 00:12:22.220
This is the reality of cruising during a pandemic.

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:26.440
As long as passengers are originating from
a place with rampant community spread, no

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:31.140
matter how many precautions are taken, it
doesn’t work from a public health perspective,

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:35.600
it doesn’t work from a business perspective,
it just simply doesn’t work.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:39.970
Therefore, for the most part, even in the
cases where governments weren’t stopping

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:42.990
them, cruise lines stopped trying.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:50.040
Nowadays, however, in Spring, 2021, tens of
millions of COVID vaccines are entering people’s

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:55.240
arms every day around the world, and a solution
has arisen.

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:59.589
Instead of trying to prevent COVID from spreading
on their ships, the cruise lines are simply

00:12:59.589 --> 00:13:04.070
going to prevent people who could have COVID
from getting on their ships.

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:08.640
Many of the major players, including Royal
Caribbean, are requiring all adult passengers

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.279
to be fully vaccinated before boarding.

00:13:11.279 --> 00:13:16.779
Even though no vaccine is 100% effective,
having a fully vaccinated ship means that

00:13:16.779 --> 00:13:22.240
even if someone does board with COVID, this
will not set off a chain reaction of community

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.190
spread throughout the entire vessel.

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:29.210
Given these new policies, cruise lines are
reworking their schedules to center their

00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:32.740
business on the places where vaccines are
rolling out the fastest.

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:37.639
For example, with less than two months notice,
Royal Caribbean announced that it would be

00:13:37.639 --> 00:13:41.940
sailing from Israel, home of the world’s
fastest vaccination campaign, for the first

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:46.529
time ever with one of the largest cruise ships
in the world—the Odyssey of the Seas.

00:13:46.529 --> 00:13:51.110
In addition, while the summer cruise season
is traditionally centered on the Mediterranean,

00:13:51.110 --> 00:13:56.480
in 2021, many of the first cruises to restart
will be based out of the Caribbean, in close

00:13:56.480 --> 00:14:02.190
proximity to the US, which has also had one
of the world’s fastest vaccination campaigns.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:06.810
Cruise lines have, for the most part, succeeded
with their grand challenge.

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:14.860
At the end of 2020, Royal Caribbean, for example,
still had $3.6 billion in the bank, as runway,

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:19.100
and had pushed their burn rate down to $270
million a month.

00:14:19.100 --> 00:14:24.779
That means they can survive without revenue
until at least February 9th, 2022.

00:14:24.779 --> 00:14:30.360
Norwegian Cruise Line, meanwhile, had enough
to make it until April 25th, 2022, while Carnival

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:34.060
had enough to last June 9th, 2022.

00:14:34.060 --> 00:14:36.890
The cruise lines will be fine.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.740
But there is another side to the equation.

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:45.620
While cruise lines are typically massive,
global enterprises, they rely on the places

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:51.399
they visit… they rely on all the small businesses
that operate tours and excursions for passengers

00:14:51.399 --> 00:14:53.199
at each port of call.

00:14:53.199 --> 00:14:57.810
Specifically, they rely on places like Ketchikan,
Alaska.

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:02.380
Last summer, under strict COVID protocols,
I travelled there with a film crew to document

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:08.070
a summer like no other—a summer where the
1.2 million annual cruise tourists that keep

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:13.300
the economy of this small town of 8,000 alive
just didn’t show up.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:17.959
Two summers ago, Ketchikan was one of the
most sought after cruise-destinations in the

00:15:17.959 --> 00:15:22.940
world—they would quite literally run out
of space on the docks.

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:28.360
Last summer, they were experiencing the most
acute form of economic ruin.

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:33.509
The documentary I made, profiling this, is
out now exclusively on Nebula.

00:15:33.509 --> 00:15:38.389
It’s the first on-location project we’ve
released in almost a year, and I think you’ll

00:15:38.389 --> 00:15:39.829
find it quite interesting.

